                     /GO_process="GO:0046170 - methanol catabolic process
                     [Evidence IEA]"
                     /note="This methanol dehydrogenase is considered a
                     nicotinoprotein, since its NADP cofactor remains is not
                     dissociable, but instead remains permanently bound. A
                     member of this family has been shown to act as a
                     formaldehyde dismutase, able to convert two molecules of
                     formaldehyde (plus one water molecule) into one of
                     methanol and one of formate, with no net change in its
                     redox state. More recently, it was shown in Mycobacterium
                     smegmatis that this enzyme is critical to ethanol
                     utilization, for which the biosynthesis of the
                     cofactor-like electron carrier mycofactocin is also
                     required; Derived by automated computational analysis
                     using gene prediction method: Protein Homology."
